# The Wayland stack, on top of wget-list-core.
#
# Separate from the core list because it is a different kind of commitment:
# the core is thirty-odd packages that make a console system, this is the
# twenty-odd that a compositor needs, and most of the time there is no reason
# to fetch it. ./download.sh gui takes only this one.
#
# Everything here is built against glibc, not musl. Mesa and LLVM are not
# realistically musl-buildable here, and a stack cannot be split between two
# C libraries, so the whole thing follows udev's lead.
#
# Not covered by md5sums, which is the LFS book's file and lists only the
# book's tarballs. Some of these have no stable checksum worth recording
# anyway: forge archive URLs are generated per request and have changed bytes
# without changing contents.
#
# An optional second field renames the downloaded file. Forge archive URLs
# end in the tag rather than the project, so without it seatd would land in
# sources/ as "0.9.1.tar.gz".
#
# Ordered the way they get built, which is dependency order.

# Tier 1.
#
# libffi is what wayland's protocol dispatch calls its callbacks through; it
# is an LFS package, but plinux never had a reason to build it until now.
# wayland is the protocol library and wayland-scanner. wayland-protocols is
# data only, the XML that the compositor and its clients generate code from.
# seatd hands out the DRM and input devices, which is what lets sway run
# without being root -- init_os already starts it if it is present, and
# shell_config.sh already sets LIBSEAT_BACKEND=seatd.
https://github.com/libffi/libffi/releases/download/v3.5.2/libffi-3.5.2.tar.gz
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wayland/wayland/-/releases/1.24.0/downloads/wayland-1.24.0.tar.xz
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wayland/wayland-protocols/-/releases/1.45/downloads/wayland-protocols-1.45.tar.xz
https://git.sr.ht/~kennylevinsen/seatd/archive/0.9.1.tar.gz  seatd-0.9.1.tar.gz

# Tier 2. The device and input layer, all of it below the compositor.
#
# libdrm is how anything talks to the kernel's DRM: mode setting, buffer
# allocation, the amdgpu ioctls. pixman is the software rasteriser wlroots
# falls back to when there is no GPU, which is what makes sway run in the VM.
# hwdata carries pnp.ids, the monitor manufacturer table libdisplay-info
# parses EDID against. xkeyboard-config is the keyboard layout data --
# "us" and "latam" are files in it -- and libxkbcommon is what reads it.
# libevdev wraps the kernel's input event protocol for libinput in tier 3.
https://dri.freedesktop.org/libdrm/libdrm-2.4.126.tar.xz
https://www.cairographics.org/releases/pixman-0.46.4.tar.gz
https://github.com/vcrhonek/hwdata/archive/v0.402/hwdata-0.402.tar.gz
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/emersion/libdisplay-info/-/archive/0.3.0/libdisplay-info-0.3.0.tar.gz
https://www.x.org/pub/individual/data/xkeyboard-config/xkeyboard-config-2.45.tar.xz
https://github.com/xkbcommon/libxkbcommon/archive/xkbcommon-1.11.0/libxkbcommon-xkbcommon-1.11.0.tar.gz  libxkbcommon-1.11.0.tar.gz
https://www.freedesktop.org/software/libevdev/libevdev-1.13.4.tar.xz

# Tier 3. libinput, and the one library it still needs.
#
# libinput turns evdev events into gestures, pointer acceleration and
# button mapping -- the behaviour a compositor is expected to have and
# should not be reimplementing. mtdev translates the kernel's older
# multitouch protocol into the current one; nothing on this machine speaks
# it, but a touchpad on whatever the rescue USB is plugged into might.
https://bitmath.org/code/mtdev/mtdev-1.1.7.tar.bz2
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/libinput/libinput/-/archive/1.30.0/libinput-1.30.0.tar.gz

# Tier 4. mesa, and no LLVM.
#
# LLVM is here only if llvmpipe is: radeonsi has not needed it since ACO,
# the AMD shader compiler Valve wrote for RADV, and mesa switches radeonsi
# to ACO on its own when LLVM is absent (si_pipe.c, #if !AMD_LLVM_AVAILABLE).
# llvmpipe is a JIT and cannot exist without one, but in the VM it would be
# software rendering -- and wlroots already has a software renderer, which is
# what pixman was built for. The saving is libLLVM, 121M unstripped on this
# host, and forty minutes of build.
https://archive.mesa3d.org/mesa-25.2.4.tar.xz

# radeonsi hard-requires libelf: mesa's meson.build errors out without it.
# elfutils is where libelf comes from.
https://sourceware.org/ftp/elfutils/0.193/elfutils-0.193.tar.bz2

# libglvnd, before mesa. Vendor-neutral dispatch: libOpenGL, libEGL and the
# GLES libraries become thin shims that route to whichever driver claims the
# device, and mesa installs itself as a vendor behind them. Without it mesa
# provides no desktop OpenGL library at all -- only GLES2 -- because its own
# libGL is gated on GLX.
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/glvnd/libglvnd/-/archive/v1.7.0/libglvnd-v1.7.0.tar.gz  libglvnd-1.7.0.tar.gz
